    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 22 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 22. Remember that effective communication is crucial in navigating the special education process. Document all interactions and keep records of all correspondence.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 6 (Serving District 22)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>5619 Flatlands Ave, Brooklyn, NY 11234</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-968-6200">718-968-6200</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Time is of the essence in special education matters. Delays can impact your child's access to needed services.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window, as outlined in NYS Part 200 Regulations 200.4(a)(1)(i)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This applies to initial referrals, requests for independent educational evaluations (IEEs), and any other formal request.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary, reiterating the points discussed and confirming any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for Independent Educational Evaluation - [Student Name]"</em>. Be specific!</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, phone numbers, email addresses, and a brief summary of each interaction. This will be invaluable if you need to escalate your concerns.</li>
                <li>Retain copies of all letters, emails, and documents sent to and received from the school district.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder. Remember to document each step. This is crucial if you need to file a formal complaint.
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request and the reason for your concern.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and the CSE Chairperson). Politely reiterate your request and mention that you are following up on your initial communication.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent (and copy the Director of Special Education for the district). State that you have not received a response to your previous emails and that you are concerned about the lack of communication. Reference IDEA's emphasis on parental involvement (20 U.S.C. § 1400(c)(5)).</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or if the district fails to adhere to mandated timelines (like conducting evaluations within 60 days of referral, as per NYS Part 200 Regulations). Instructions on filing a state complaint can be found on the NYSED website. You can also consider contacting the New York State Education Department's Problem Resolution System (PRS).</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 22
    </h2>

    <div class="tip-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #28a745;">Know Your Rights</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. Understanding your rights is the first step to effective advocacy. Pay special attention to procedural safeguards, which outline your rights to participate in meetings, access records, and challenge decisions.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #dc3545;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain detailed records of all communications, meetings, and evaluations. This documentation will be crucial if you need to escalate your concerns or pursue legal remedies. Use a dedicated notebook or electronic folder for this purpose.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #ffc107;">Be Prepared for CSE Meetings</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Before each Committee on Special Education (CSE) meeting, review your child's IEP (Individualized Education Program), evaluations, and progress reports. Prepare a list of questions and concerns you want to address. Bring a friend, advocate, or attorney for support. Under IDEA, you have the right to bring anyone you choose to the CSE meeting (20 U.S.C. § 1414(d)(1)(B)).
            </p>
        </div>

         <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #17a2b8;">Request Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you disagree with the school district's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense. The district must either pay for the IEE or demonstrate at a due process hearing that their evaluation was appropriate. (34 CFR § 300.502). Knowing how to request an IEE is paramount.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #6c757d;">Understand the IEP</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The IEP is the cornerstone of your child's special education program. Make sure you understand each section of the IEP, including present levels of performance, goals, services, and accommodations. If you have questions, don't hesitate to ask the CSE team for clarification. Ensure the goals are SMART: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What is the difference between the CSE and the CPSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                A: The Committee on Special Education (CSE) handles special education services for students aged 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) handles services for children aged 3-5. In NYC District 22, you'll primarily be working with the CSE as most children are over the age of 5.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: How long does the evaluation process take in NYC?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                A: According to NYS Part 200 Regulations, the school district has 60 school days from the date they receive your written consent for evaluation to complete the evaluation and hold a CSE meeting to discuss the results and determine eligibility for special education services (200.4(a)(1)(i)). This timeline can be a point of contention, so proactive follow-up is crucial.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What should I do if I disagree with the IEP developed by the CSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                A: You have several options if you disagree with the IEP. First, express your concerns to the CSE team and attempt to reach a compromise. You can request another CSE meeting to further discuss your concerns. If you are still not satisfied, you can pursue mediation or file a due process complaint to challenge the IEP. You also have the right to file a State Complaint with NYSED.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What is an impartial hearing, and when should I request one?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                A: An impartial hearing (due process hearing) is a formal legal proceeding where an independent hearing officer hears evidence and makes a decision about a dispute between you and the school district regarding your child's special education program. You should request an impartial hearing if you have exhausted other methods of resolving the dispute and believe that the district has violated your child's rights under IDEA. There are strict timelines for filing and resolving due process complaints, so it's essential to seek legal advice promptly.
            </p>
        </div>

         <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What if my child needs services not available in District 22?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                A: If the CSE determines that your child requires services not available within District 22, the district is responsible for providing those services. This may involve contracting with outside agencies, placing your child in a specialized school, or providing related services such as speech therapy or occupational therapy from qualified providers outside the district. The IEP must clearly specify how these services will be provided and funded.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>


    </div>
</main>
